.Dd November 9, 2026
.Dt MEM 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mem
.Nd physical memory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/mem
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Pa /dev/mem
is the physical address space, from 0 to 4 GiB, as Linux gives it with
.Dv STRICT_DEVMEM :
a byte at an offset of the file is the byte at that physical address.
A privileged process reads and writes it with
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr write 2 ,
.Xr pread 2
and
.Xr pwrite 2 ,
seeks it with
.Xr lseek 2 ,
and maps it with
.Xr mmap 2 ,
where BusyBox's
.Ic devmem
reaches a device's registers.
.Pp
It reaches the first megabyte, the frames the memory map does not offer
as RAM, which firmware and devices have, and the addresses past the top
of memory; another frame of RAM, the kernel's or a process's, is
.Er EPERM .
A read or a write that comes to such a frame stops before it, and fails
with
.Er EPERM
when it is the first.
.Pp
A mapping must be shared.
Its pages are mapped at their first touch, with the processor's cache
disabled for them, so a load or a store reaches the device.
A writable mapping needs a descriptor open for writing, and one through a
descriptor open for reading alone may never be made writable.
A child of
.Xr fork 2
shares the pages, unless
.Xr madvise 2 Ns 's
.Dv MADV_DONTFORK
marked the range, which then is not mapped in the child.
The mapping may shrink and move with
.Xr mremap 2 ,
but not grow, which fails with
.Er EFAULT ;
.Xr madvise 2
does not let its pages go, nor clear the fork mark with
.Dv MADV_DOFORK ,
and fails with
.Er EINVAL ;
and a core file and
.Pa /proc/PID/mem
leave its bytes out, since reading a device's register can change the
device;
.Pa /proc/PID/maps
shows it shared, at the physical address it maps, and
.Pa /proc/PID/smaps
gives it the flags
.Ql io
and
.Ql pf .
.Pp
The mapping is the first of an object that maps itself through its
.Xr mmap 2
operation, as the DRM devices and dma-bufs do.
.Sh FILES
.Pa /dev/mem
has mode 0640, owner and group root, and major 1, minor 1.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EINVAL
.It Er EACCES
A writable mapping through a descriptor not open for writing, or
.Xr mprotect 2
asking write of a mapping made through one.
.It Er EINVAL
A private mapping.
.It Er EPERM
The caller is not privileged, or the range has a frame of RAM past the
first megabyte, or, at the open, the kernel is locked down
.Pq see Xr kernel_lockdown 7 .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr busybox-miscutils 1 ,
.Xr madvise 2 ,
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr null 4 ,
.Xr kernel_lockdown 7
